<speak version="1.0" xml:lang="en-US" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xmlns:amazon="http://amazon.com/schemas/ssml-1.0">
<amazon:domain name="conversational">

<mark name="s01_intro"/>
It's seven in the morning in a busy home. What's due today? What's running low? Are we still on budget? HomeOps is a self-hosted <say-as interpret-as="characters">MCP</say-as> server that lets <sub alias="Alexa plus">Alexa+</sub> answer all of it, and act on it, in one conversation.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s02_briefing_lead"/>
One question gets the whole morning: today's tasks, whose turn it is for chores, what's running low, and the budget, chained into a single tool call.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s04_capture_lead"/>
Nobody needs to know which tool does what. HomeOps sorts a plain sentence into the shopping list, a task, or an expense.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s06_reorder_lead"/>
Before it spends anything, HomeOps asks. <sub alias="Alexa plus">Alexa+</sub> puts a real confirmation in front of you, and nothing is ordered without a yes.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s08_alerts_lead"/>
And it speaks up before problems happen. That grocery run puts the week on pace to blow far past its budget, so HomeOps flags it now, not at the end of the week.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s10_aws_arch"/>
Under the hood, HomeOps runs on Amazon <say-as interpret-as="characters">ECS</say-as> Express Mode, deployed from <say-as interpret-as="characters">AWS</say-as> CloudShell with one command. When a client can't run its own model, it calls Claude on Amazon Bedrock through a least-privilege role.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s11_tests"/>
Every tool is tested end to end against a real <say-as interpret-as="characters">MCP</say-as> client: forty-one checks, including the Bedrock path.
<break time="700ms"/>

<mark name="s12_close"/>
HomeOps. A household agent that asks before it spends, and speaks up before problems happen.
<break time="700ms"/>

</amazon:domain>
</speak>
